 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Self-hosted WebSockets for change delivery.** Committed revisions are pushed to connected browsers over server-held WebSocket sessions rather than five-second HTTPS polling or a hosted realtime service. Polling was rejected: it repeats reads and adds seconds of lag for the same freshness target. A hosted realtime service was rejected: recurring fees and provider coupling outweigh the connection operations it would offload. Self-hosting accepts connection-capacity and reconnect work in exchange for low-latency updates with no realtime vendor dependency. See [Change delivery route](decisions/change-delivery-route.md).
